  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/4/4f/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_27.png",
  "plot_summary": "The FF are marveling at Reed's new thought-projector device, but Sue excuses herself before someone suggests she try it.&nbsp; Reed then admits to Ben and Johnny that he is about to go searching for an engagement ring.\n\nBut little does he know that \"at that very moment,\" the Sub-Mariner is lamenting that \"a prince must have a bride to <i>share</i> his throne!\" and \"If I could have the surface girl I love at my side, nothing else would matter!\"&nbsp; When he announces his intentions to his last few remaining loyal subjects, they disagree and then take off, commenting that, \"He left us no choice!&nbsp; We <i>must</i> desert him!&nbsp; We cannot be loyal to a monarch who would betray his royal heritage!\"&nbsp; So Namor is alone again - forced to follow his heart.\n\nAnd soon, the Sub-Mariner attacks!&nbsp; He quickly dispatches the Thing and Human Torch, then tells Sue why he has come.&nbsp; She warns him that Reed will find out (when he returns) and come after him, to which he replies \"Richards has had <i>years</i> to court you, to make you love him!&nbsp; All I ask is 24 hours, and I shall <i>have </i>them!!\"&nbsp; And he <i>gasses</i> her to sleep.&nbsp; As he soars off with her, he muses, \"...she <i>must</i> marry me!&nbsp; She <i>must</i>!&nbsp; Or else I have sacrificed everything in vain!\"\n\nReed soon returns with engagement ring in hand, only to learn from Ben and Johnny what has happened.&nbsp; Reed uncharacteristically flies off the handle, completely enraged, and snaps at them, \"Namor is <i>mine!</i>  He's stolen the one thing I love most in the world!&nbsp; ...<i>Nothing</i> will save him now!\"&nbsp; And Reed heads off to his \"radar-sweep-ray\" to find Namor, with Ben concluding that they've \"got to find a way to reach the Sub-Mariner <i>first</i>!\"\n\nBut when Ben points out they don't have \"any of the hocus-pocus stuff of Reed's to help us find him,\" it gives Johnny an idea of \"the <i>one</i> person who might be able to find Namor...the one man who <i>knows</i> hocus-pocus!\"&nbsp; And he sky-writes a message to \"Dr. Strange\" to contact him.\n\nAnd sure enough, in his Greenwich Village sanctuary, Dr. Strange takes note and sends his \"ectoplasmic form\" to the Baxter Building, where he sees the departure of Reed and then learns from Ben and Johnny about what's going on.&nbsp; He agrees to help find the Sub-Mariner and sends his shadowy form into the depths, soon locating \"a magnificent ornate castle.\"\n\nInside, Sue declares to Namor, \"Do you think you can make me confess I love you by <i>imprisoning</i> me?\"&nbsp; But Namor explains he's had no chance to date her the way Richards has.&nbsp; \"All I ask is 24 hours...\"&nbsp; Sue is having none of it and indicates that once Reed finds her, \"<i>nothing</i> can save you!\"\n\nDr. Strange has seen all and departs to tell Ben and Johnny, but notices Reed heading inside at the same time as his shadowy form withdraws.&nbsp; Reed attacks Namor, who declares \"<i>This</i> time there are no others to help you!&nbsp; <i>This</i> time you die!\"&nbsp; And as Reed and Namor battle, they are interrupted by the sudden return of a squad of undersea warriors, who declare, \"We realized we could <i>not</i> desert our prince and still be worthy of the name sub-mariners!\" Reed is now cornered, but he's determined to \"finish you off forever!\"\n\nMeanwhile, Dr. Strange returns to the Baxter Building to inform the Thing and Human Torch, and once he returns in his actual, physical body, he promises to \"send you to Prince Namor by the fastest way imaginable!\"&nbsp; And before they have time to blink, the pair are there with Reed, watching him be attacked by Namor's \"deep-sea stooges havin' target practice with our bouncin' buddy!\"&nbsp; And they join in on the fray.\n\nNamor is fine with them being there, as it will allow him to destroy all of them at once.&nbsp; But Reed still is enraged, saying, \"I'm going to make him wish he'd never <i>heard</i> of the human race!\"&nbsp; Namor is actually surprised to see Reed so passionate and thinks that perhaps he now finally understands his own feelings of losing his kingdom -- everything he holds dear... \"because of you accursed <i>humans</i>!\"&nbsp; But Reed doesn't care.&nbsp; \"So long as you live, mankind will be in danger!!&nbsp; I'll see to it that Namor the <i>First</i> becomes Namor the <i>Last</i>!\"\n\nBen handles the guards while Johnny flies off to free Sue, while meanwhile, Namor and Reed continue to battle it out.&nbsp; Reed's pliable body is able to withstand Namor's far stronger blows, until Namor finally realizes that \"We live by different codes!&nbsp; They've <i>never</i> understood me!&nbsp; They'll <i>always </i>hound me... unless I stop them now... stop them forever!\"&nbsp; He has his men aim their undersea blast gun at Reed and Ben and offers them the \"privileges of prisoners of war\" if they surrender unconditionally.&nbsp; Of course they refuse, and Namor unleashes the gun, but it mysteriously does no harm!&nbsp; Sue's invisible force field has protected them!\n\nNamor challenges her on the spot to choose between himself and Reed, only to hear Sue state for the record that, \"Reed is the only man for me... nothing can ever change that!\"&nbsp; This sends Namor into an outrage, but just as he prepares to strike, an onlooking Dr. Strange sees the major escalation that is about to take place, sees that \"the sea prince has never been so scorned before!&nbsp; His rage is mounting!&nbsp; He is capable of <i>anything</i>!\"&nbsp; He transports the FF to safety, even as Namor gives the command to seize them!\n\nReed is still unsure that Sue actually <i>meant</i> what she said about loving him.&nbsp; Did she <i>mean </i>it, or did she just say that to prevent any further fighting?\n\nBut we see Sue's thoughts, \"Oh, Reed, you blind fool!&nbsp; Of <i>course</i> it's you I love!!&nbsp; But how can I ever <i>convince</i> you?&nbsp; You'll always suspect I may have just been trying to protect you... or Namor!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | PopTheQuestion | width=12%] <b>FIXME</b>\n\nWhile it's true that Namor's last appearance in <i>this</i> magazine was in last summer's annual ([LINK:1963:FF_Annual_1|FF Annual 1]), it's not as if readers hadn't had a more recent peek at some of his activities.&nbsp; Remember that he joined up with the Hulk in [LINK:1963:AVEN_3|AVEN #3|2027-03-01] (back in November), which ultimately led to him freeing Captain America in that title's following issue (the legendary [LINK:1964:AVEN_4|AVEN #4|2027-04-25], published only a couple of months ago), where we also learned that he <i>did</i> still have a handful of warrior troops who remained loyal to him.&nbsp; They even fled back to the ocean <i>with</i> the Sub-Mariner at the end of that adventure, believing (erroneously) that the Avengers were finished.\n\nSo it comes as no surprise that when he finally appears in <i>this</i> issue, he mentions that he's still <i>mostly</i> alone, only having \"a few faithful warriors\" that remain at his side.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=(And by the way, kudos to Stan and Jack for the accurate follow-through on the continuity here.)]\n\n[ASSET: left | PrincessSue | width=20%] [ASSET: right | AbandonedAgain | width=17%] Yet when the narrative joins him on page 3, he has decided to try once again to make Sue Storm his princess because he feels that having a princess by his side is the only way a ruler like him can eventually reunite his undersea empire and cement himself as \"Prince Namor.\"&nbsp; (And in this, he is obviously misguided, because as the annual showed, it was <i>because</i> of his feelings for the \"surface girl\" that Lady Dorma and the rest of the Atlanteans abandoned him in the <i>first place</i>.)\n[BR]\n[BR] The moment he announces this plan to his few remaining subjects, they take off, proving that Namor simply hasn't learned his lesson!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But also freeing him up to battle the FF once more.&nbsp; It <i>is</i> the FF's magazine after all, and that's apparently all that matters!)]\n\n[ASSET: left | Claiming24hours | width=13%] The Sub-Mariner's arrival at the Baxter Building, battle with the Thing and Human Torch, followed by his subsequent kidnapping of Sue, all while telling her that Reed Richards has <i>had</i> his chance to court her...\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=The <i>audacity</i> of it all actually made me laugh!]\n[BR]\n[BR] Oh <i>sure</i>!&nbsp; Gas the girl to sleep, haul her off, and demand that she confess her undying love toward him!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Hahahaha!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Not much on romance, are you?&nbsp; <i>Geez!</i>)]\n\nThis only shows how <i>little</i> Namor has learned since he first laid eyes on Sue back in [LINK:1962:FF_4|FF #4] (published over two years ago), where he offered to \"show mercy to the rest of your pitiful race\" if she would only be his bride.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=Failing that, <i>clearly</i> the next step in his mind is to resort to kidnapping!]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=large; fonttype=italic; text=Holy plot acceleration, Batman!]\n[BR] (And yet, it's all done with a straight <i>face</i>, no less!&nbsp; You almost can't make this up!)\n\nIt continues to <i>amaze</i> me that Namor is so arrogant that he believes he can kidnap a woman and take only 24 hours to sway her into falling in love with him and agreeing to be his bride.&nbsp; This plot was so hurriedly thrust on the reader that it barrels forward at breakneck speed.\n\nIt takes only one page from Subby's initial appearance in the story before he storms into the Baxter Building and kidnaps his intended princess!&nbsp; (And it makes me wonder if readers didn't actually page back in the story, thinking \"Did I <i>miss</i> something?\")\n\nOnce again, when it comes to Namor:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=Subtlety Sold Separately!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I <i>know</i> there are a limited number of pages, but <i>geez</i>!&nbsp; I think another page or two devoted to setup might have been <i>okay</i>!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n<i>Naturally</i>, it's all done while Reed is off buying the engagement ring, which is simply just the latest episode of [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Comic Book Convenience 101]!\n\n\nReed's out-of-control rage was eye-opening.&nbsp; I understand that he was mad with rage due to the audacity of Namor thinking he could steal his girl away from him, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=but to be prepared to finish him off for <i>good</i>] -- perhaps his intelligence would have prevented him from striking the fatal blow had it actually come to that, but <i>boy</i> was he ever pissed!\n\n\nThe guest appearance of Doctor Strange was fine, with the exception of how he was initially summoned.&nbsp; Johnny sent out the request for help with only the hint that a \"Dr. Strange\" even existed.&nbsp; Readers can only assume that Johnny knew of him because they share a monthly magazine, namely [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Strange Tales] [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=(proving that Marvel isn't above winking directly at its audience)].\n[ASSET: center | CallingDrStrange | width=large]\n\nThe guest star had very little to do, but I think that was the point.&nbsp; He was presumably only there to boost sales.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=Actually, he had one <i>more</i> use, which was to magically transport the FF away from the enraged Namor when the page count ran out.]\n\n[ASSET: left | AnotherWayToHelp | width=18%] [ASSET: right | SpecialPill | width=12%] To Stan's credit, he <i>at least</i> has Dr. Strange ponder how different it is to be involved with the more mundane ways of helping mankind.&nbsp; At the very least, by agreeing to help the Fantastic Four, Strange shows that he does not condone Sue's kidnapping.\n[BR]\n[BR] And yet, when he spots Reed about to storm in on Namor, I suspect he doesn't condone that <i>either</i>!\n\nAlso, having Strange whisk them away to safety kept this to a one-issue story instead of it extending into the next issue (which would also have been okay -- and might have helped with some of the very-rushed characterization).&nbsp; This was a case where there was simply too much plot to cover and too few pages to cram it into.\n\n[ASSET: center | ReedIsPissed | width=large]\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n\n[ASSET: left | IfOnlyICouldBreakFree | width=14%] Page 12 shows Sue watching Reed and Namor trade blows, but she thinks,\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"If only I could break free before they kill each other!\"]\n[BR]\n[BR] I confess I had to scratch my head here, wanting to point out,\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=\"Sue, you don't <i>need</i> to break free!&nbsp; Just use your force field power to intervene!\"]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But both Stan and Jack seem to have forgotten that -- she helps only after Johnny frees her on page 16.)]\n\n\n[ASSET: left | PowerOfLove | width=12%] Pages 14-21 were pretty much all-out war, which I'm sure suited Jack Kirby just fine, leading to Namor's final ultimatum, demanding that Sue make her choice.\n[BR]\n[BR] Yet before that, on page 18, I liked how the Thing was juggling the other Atlanteans and pretty much eating popcorn and watching Reed and Namor duke it out.&nbsp; I'm sure it must have been <i>quite</i> gratifying for him to watch Reed lose control that way, when normally <i>he</i> is the one Reed and company have to hold back.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=Very nice.]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n\n[ASSET: left | RoughArtwork | width=12%] The artwork in this issue was sub-par, particularly with the images of the Thing, whose look appeared more rough, less detailed.&nbsp; It might have been the inking of \"Geo. Bell\" (George \"Bell\" Roussos).&nbsp; Most of the characters looked okay in this issue, but Reed and the Thing in particular looked less defined and hurried, and with a scene-stealing presence such as the Thing, that's probably something they should get right.&nbsp; I found it distracting.\n\nFinally, what's with the Sub-Mariner's red swimming trunks on the cover?&nbsp; His trunks have always been green before, and this is the only place in the magazine where they aren't!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=Oops (perhaps)?]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n\nA right-about-mid-grade read.\n\nI have to confess that I enjoyed the opening moments of the book, largely because of its unintended comedy.\n\nIt's difficult to read this story without bursting out laughing:\n[HANG] * first because of Namor's absolute <b><i>cluelessness</i></b><i> </i>about how to woo a woman,\n[HANG] * to the abject <b><i>overreaction</i></b> of Reed Richards,\n[HANG] * to the <b><i>meta-awareness</i></b> of the Human Torch about his solo magazine's co-star,\n[HANG] * to <b>FIXME</b>\n\nThis was clearly a case where there was too much plot to cram into a single book.&nbsp; I think the story could have been better had Stan and Jack paused to let the story unfold more organically.&nbsp; You'd <i>just done</i> a two-parter in the last couple of issues, showing how you <i>could</i> take your time, but my guess is that Stan hadn't yet heard back from readers as to whether they actually <i>liked</i> the idea of the two-part story, and so decided to finish this one off in a single issue.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=It just wasn't long enough -- it could have had more impact had it been allowed to breathe a little more.]\n\nSpeaking of <i>breathing</i>, I was a <i>little</i> annoyed that first Reed, then Ben and Johnny, had <i>extremely convenient</i> \"underwater breathin' pills\" at their disposal that allowed them to (according to Reed) \"do without oxygen for one full hour!\"&nbsp; The existence of such pills would seem an eminently saleable invention and thus completely eliminate all of the Fantastic Four's financial problems, likely <b><i>forever</i></b>, but does he share this technology with the general public?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Heck no!])&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But... <i>hey</i>... it's a comic book after all!)]\n\nThe book was okay.&nbsp; I found it rushed way too much, pushed the limits of Reed's personality to near breaking point, all the while allowing enough room for its main guest-star to briefly shine (but also using him to prevent the story from spilling over to another issue, which might be viewed as a bit of a cop-out).&nbsp; But mostly, I think it diminished the character of the Sub-Mariner.&nbsp; Namor went from a near-sympathetic figure to just a downright arrogant jackass.&nbsp; It was perhaps a bit much, and thus the book is capped at mid-grade -- too much going against it (including the distracting art) to elevate it above that.\n\nDespite a host of problems and rushed pace, the book actually made me laugh, likely <i>because</i> of said outrageous pace.&nbsp; I didn't think the guest star added much in the way of actual help (only providing transportation when all was said and done), but Namor's cockamamie scheme to win a girl by kidnapping her, Reed's over-the-top reaction, Johnny's <i>wink-wink-nod-nod</i> at the audience, and the underwater free-for-all was entertaining enough to earn a better-than-mid-grade rating in the end.\n\nBut it should have been longer.&nbsp; That was <i>entirely</i> too much plot to cram into the allotted space.",
  "side_note": [
    "This is, of course, the very first crossover story involving Dr. Strange.&nbsp; Up until now, he has been quietly occupying his own corner of the Marvel Universe (something that I've actually commented on).&nbsp; While it's nice to know he isn't on an island by himself, I found his use here far more perfunctory, as if done <i>only</i> to reinforce to readers that he actually <i>does</i> co-exist with the other Marvel characters.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=That's <i>something</i>, but... was it enough?]"
  ],
  "letters_page": "Gregory Suriano says \"I have to congratulate you on those witty little 'remarks' you sometimes insert into the boxes with the names of the artists, writer and letterer.\"&nbsp; <i>True enough</i>!&nbsp; I agree that those small things always help make <i>me </i>smile as well!&nbsp; Goofy?&nbsp; <i>Sure</i>!&nbsp; But fun as well.\n\nJim Grondin's letter applauds Marvel for its various magazines and ends his letter with \"I also think that you are very conceited!\" -- to which the editor replies:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=Jim, we can't understand why people seem to think that we talented Marvel geniuses are conceited!]&nbsp; (<i>Heh</i>, you gotta love it!)",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}